    Everything below it on that page belongs to the next section, and the
    boundary is in the SAME column rather than at the column break: the Skills
    line is followed by a full-width illustration and then by the heading
    "Creating More Alien Races", still in the left column, the nine-step
    generator that is not a class. The right column of 104 opens with unheaded
    prose continuing that section. A first draft of this note put the heading
    in the right column, which is the reading the OCR''s linear order suggests
    and the render refutes. The survey''s range of 102-104 is correct as printed
    and was re-derived rather than inherited.
  - THE BOOK CONTRADICTS ITS OWN EXPERIENCE TABLE AND THE TABLE WINS. Printed
    103 says level improvement continues using the Fallen Knight Experience
    Table. THERE IS NO SUCH TABLE. Printed 183 was rendered whole and carries
    FOURTEEN ladders; one is headed `Cosmo-Knight` and none is headed Fallen,
    and `Fallen` appears nowhere in the OCR of printed 183 - it is in the cache
    only on printed 5, 6, 96 and 99-104. The alphabetical index on printed 6
    files this entry as "Cosmo-Knight: Fallen O.C.C." at page 102, a sub-entry
    of the Cosmo-Knight rather than a class of its own. So the PROSE LOST and
    the table won, and the fallen knight shares the Cosmo-Knight ladder, which
    is what is stored here. This is the Royal Kreeghor situation with the signs
    reversed: there a section heading corrected a survey built from the
    Contents; here a table corrects a sentence.
  - IT IS PLAYABLE, AND ITS OWN SUBHEADING SAYS BOTH THINGS AT ONCE. The
    heading on printed 102 reads "Optional Player Character or NPC Villains" -
    a label neither the Royal Kreeghor rule (a heading carrying the NPC label
    the Contents does not) nor the Vacuum Wasp rule (playable entries
    interleaved with NPC ones) covers, because it is both labels in one line.
    Printed 103 settles it four paragraphs later with a bold line of its own:
    available as player characters. The index on printed 6 agrees, calling it
    an O.C.C. and attaching no NPC label, where it does label the Second Stage
    Promethean and three others NPC. Two authorities for playable, none
    against.
  - IT IS TWO CLASSES AND NOT A VARIANT, AND `VARIANT_OVERRIDES` IS WHY. The
    entry is printed entirely as deltas off the Cosmo-Knight - use its
    attributes but reduce them, halve the M.D.C., halve the P.P.E., same skills
    at -20% - which reads exactly like `variants`. It cannot be one.
    `VARIANT_OVERRIDES` in `js/parser.js` is a literal list, grepped rather
    than assumed: `attribute_dice`, `attribute_requirements`, the four pool
    bases, `starting_money`, `bonuses` and `skill_overrides`. `special_abilities`
    is NOT among them, so a variant INHERITS the base''s abilities wholesale -
    and the sentence this entry turns on is that ALL former cosmo-knight powers
    and O.C.C. bonuses are lost. A variant would have shipped a fallen knight
    still carrying resistance-to-energy-divided-by-100, superluminal flight,
    cosmic blasts, galactic awareness, the 500-point cosmic armor and the +2/+2
    bonus block: every power the entry exists to take away. `skill_overrides`
    could restate the seven O.C.C. skills at -20% and could not touch the ten
    related picks or the six secondary ones, so even the half that fits fits
    only partly. Two classes, on the draconid''s precedent from batch 7.
  - THE ATTRIBUTE DICE ARE THE COSMO-KNIGHT''S WITH THE PRINTED DELTAS ALREADY
    APPLIED - I.Q. -2, M.E. -6, M.A. -4, P.S. -22, P.P. -6, P.E. -6, Spd. -10,
    and no reduction printed for P.B., which therefore keeps 3D6. The
    take-the-higher rule the Cosmo-Knight states is inherited with the
    attributes and is equally unstorable; see BOOK-INGEST-AUDIT.md F11, whose
    measurements cover this class too.
  - THE HALVED POOLS ARE ARITHMETIC ON A PRINTED FIGURE, NOT A GUESS, AND THE
    HALVES ARE EXACT. Half of the Cosmo-Knight''s 4D6x10+60 is stored as
    2D6x10+30: identical range, 50 to 150 against half of 100 to 300, and
    identical mean, 100 against half of 200. Half of 1D6x100 is stored as
    1D6x50: identical range and mean again. The per-level 4D6 is halved to 2D6
    the same way. `mdc_base` and `ppe_base` accept only a dice grammar with no
    division in it, so the alternative was storing the cosmo-knight''s full
    figures and describing the halving in prose, which would have put a
    fallen knight on the sheet at twice the toughness the book gives him.
  - NO `Money:` LINE AND NO EQUIPMENT LIST. Grepped across printed 99 to 104:
    neither cosmo-knight entry prints one. The Cosmo-Knight''s Standard
    Equipment is prose naming no item, and this entry has no equipment line at
    all. So there is no `starting_money` and no `equipment_starting`, and
    `--field-sources` has nothing to check on this class rather than having
    missed something.
  - THE MAGIC-OR-PSIONICS OPTION IS DESCRIBED AND NOT GRANTED. The book points
    at the ley line walker and the mind melter for progression and withholds
    their special abilities, and adds two classes from books this catalog does
    not hold. A `magic` or `psionics` block would have to replicate one of two
    other classes'' whole ladders and then pick between them at creation, which
    nothing here can express; the Time Master''s temporal magic in batch 9 is
    the precedent for granting less rather than granting an ungated
    approximation. Both named classes are in this catalog and a player can read
    them.
  - THIS CLASS COST THE REGRESSION SUITE ONE PINNED INVARIANT, AND IT IS THE
    ONLY CODE THIS BATCH TOUCHED. `regression.mjs` required every language
    choice group to carry a bonus GREATER THAN ZERO. A fallen knight''s is
    exactly zero - the Cosmo-Knight''s +20% less this entry''s -20% - and the
    pick then resolves at the catalog''s own 50% +5%/level, which is the right
    answer. The check''s own comment says what it was written to catch: a bonus
    LOST in a rewrite, which arrives as `undefined` rather than as 0. So the
    comparison moved from `> 0` to `>= 0` and the check still catches every
    case it was built for, while no longer asserting something the books do not
    guarantee. The zero is written out explicitly here so an absent bonus and a
    computed one still read differently. Called out because the batch''s
    standing rule is that a book changes no code, and this is the exception -
    it is a TEST rather than the app, the schema, a validator or a generator,
    and it is one operator.
  - THE -20% DOES NOT REACH THE SECONDARY SKILLS, because `parser.js` rejects a
    bonus on a secondary category outright - the comment there reasons that a
    book''s parenthetical percentage applies to related selections only, which
    is true of every class before this one and is not true of a blanket
    penalty. Recorded in the secondary-skills note so a GM can apply it.
  - THIS CLASS NEEDS NO `CORE_SDC_BY_CLASS` ENTRY: `withCorePools` returns early
    for a class stating an `mdc_base`, and this one states 2D6x10+30.
