# Fallen Cosmo-Knight

**Optional player character or NPC villain. Available as a player character.**

## Lore

A character who has forsaken the cosmo-knights'' code is both pitiable and
fearsome. He loses most of his cosmic power, much of his self-respect and
sometimes his sanity, and he remembers what he had the way a recently blinded
man remembers color. Many sink into bitterness and despair. Those who do not
kill themselves or vanish into hermitage have to decide what to do with the rest
of a very long life, and a great many turn into the exact opposite of what they
were.

Fallen knights are dangerous: supernaturally strong and durable still, often
endowed with magic or psionics afterwards, and knowing more about cosmo-knights
and their weaknesses than anyone alive. Some become psychotic and take pleasure
in inflicting pain and chaos, especially on knights who did not fall. Others
become savage vigilantes who kill criminals - or anyone they suspect of being
one - without much concern for law or bystanders. Others still are simply
self-serving, chasing wealth and new power. And some try to earn back what they
lost, or at least their self-respect, by going on serving the cause of light and
making amends; a few of those are more heroic and more noble than they ever were
as cosmo-knights.

## The road back

Regaining the power is near impossible. Most give up after a few decades. Of
those who keep trying, roughly one in a thousand succeeds, and in all of
recorded history only a dozen or so have redeemed themselves to the Cosmic
Forge''s satisfaction and gone on to become legends. Many die heroically in the
attempt without ever getting there. Some regain their powers only for as long as
it takes to spend them - confronted with impossible odds, restored just long
enough to sacrifice themselves for a good cause or to buy other lives with their
own.

## Playing one

A fallen knight player character starts at first, second or third level, which
means he was a beginner cosmo-knight who lost his powers early. The player
should say why - the GM can help - and what the character wants now, whether
that is good, selfish, vengeful or evil. He can be a tragic hero, a dedicated
champion of good, a self-serving miscreant, a bitter and angry vigilante, or
something else entirely, and the background makes a serviceable springboard for
adventures and whole campaigns.

## GM Notes

- **NPC fallen knights are typically 5th to 8th level.** A character who falls
  during play loses all experience points above what his current level needs
  and goes on from there. The book says level improvement then continues on a
  Fallen Knight Experience Table, and there is no such table in the book -
  printed 183 gives the pair a single ladder, headed Cosmo-Knight, which is the
  one stored here.
- **Apply -20% to secondary skills.** The sheet cannot carry it; the class''s
  O.C.C. and related skills already have it.
- **The cosmic weapon is not granted.** A fallen knight has one only if the
  character forged one before the fall. It keeps its 1D4x10 M.D. and its return
  flight, at half throwing range and with no bought power.
- **Magic or psionics is a choice made after the fall**, at first level, using
  the ley line walker''s or the mind melter''s progression for spells or powers
  only - none of those classes'' special abilities.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'fallen-cosmo-knight');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'fallen-cosmo-knight';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-fallen-cosmo-knight-class.sql');
